{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a 'rational preference' based on critical discussion, not inductive probability. It relies on the meta-scientific goal of truth-likeness (verisimilitude) rather than predictive success derived from past uniformity.","counterconditions":["If the agent implicitly assumes that surviving past tests increases the likelihood of surviving future tests.","If the concept of 'verisimilitude' cannot be defined without relying on probabilistic assumptions.","If the preference is shown to be functionally identical to Bayesian updating in practice."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Critical Rationalism distinguishes between the logical probability of a theory (which remains zero or undefined for universal laws) and the rational decision to prefer the theory that has withstood the most severe tests. This preference is a procedural rule for error elimination, not an inductive inference that the future will resemble the past.","optional_refs":["51ef8f97a9c0ad0ac451cd6c221e1e92ab89fa2614971e29531d63b83d1904ca","49b933dadc62b63b59098c57873624285a0b24eade8b3a8d1fc6a59fb36c6105","86cfe1115fc93ff16913b0f137fa284bb68649be4a404aba5ef19d42472901fb"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.92},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories inevitably smuggles induction back into Popper's system. Any practical reliance on past test survival to guide future action constitutes an inductive principle, regardless of Popper's verbal denials.","counterconditions":["If a non-inductive justification for acting on past success can be rigorously formulated.","If the preference can be shown to be purely conventional with no claim to reliability.","If 'rationality' can be defined entirely independently of expected success."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Pragmatic Induction: While Popper denies the logical validity of induction, his prescription to 'prefer the better-tested theory' functions as an inductive rule in practice. If past performance is not taken as evidence for future performance, the preference is arbitrary; if it is, then induction is present by definition.","optional_refs":["f759e1797c3c7358c9f05eca44bdc692d699cccb94f075e8e216f35bb555d77f","605ff1954b616ea5b2ed456495e8952a3412cf9422e614619b532c73a5f4144d","a3bf6919875f1a65f41c635d6bf238f7adc614f3b772c3a0c9103d893d75e8cc"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.88},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is defensible only if accepted as a 'conventionalist' decision to adopt a specific methodology (falsificationism) for the sake of scientific growth, abandoning the claim that this methodology tracks truth or probability.","counterconditions":["If Popper explicitly claims the methodology leads to truth (verisimilitude) rather than just faster falsification.","If the preference cannot be separated from the hope of predictive success.","If 'growth' itself relies on an inductive assumption that new theories will be better."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Methodological Conventionalism: Scientists prefer corroborated theories not because they are likely true or will succeed, but because this rule maximizes the speed of error detection. It is a self-imposed rule of the game of science, not a description of reality or a logical necessity.","optional_refs":["5ef493f2384fa5d512512bd3a92bd5fb87af4d88d07cebcea1440e1fca07971d","8653a93a35d43191d6518c7629ca6b147480c93101961b1f816e7f6208016204"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The dilemma dissolves when viewing corroboration as a measure of 'informational content' survived, not probability. Preference is for the theory with the highest empirical content that has not yet been refuted, which is a logical relation, not an inductive one.","counterconditions":["If scientists actually care more about safety/probability than informational content.","If 'surviving tests' cannot be decoupled from 'likely to survive future tests'.","If high content necessarily implies low survivability in the long run."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Content-Based Selection: Corroboration reports how much risky content a theory has survived. We prefer high-content survivors because they tell us more about the world, even if we assign them zero probability. The preference is for informativeness, not likelihood.","optional_refs":["d17b1621d338e4243a8aea2897eb8fc7a91873f1b78c220abd92f049f8ed6d67","0e7c744798ee87399a050abd6cd2e04b6bc10eca0bf07854be5e563dd7989460"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.78}],"simulation_proposals":[]}